| # The blanks a `.ONESHELL` line ends on are script text wherever the shell is
| # reading text rather than splitting words, and a double-quoted string that
| # spans lines is the second of the places it is. The quote opened on an EARLIER
| # line of the same script, so nothing about this line says its trailing blank is
| # inside one: a translation that reads a line at a time cannot see the quote,
| # and the only answer that is right whether or not it can is to leave the end of
| # every line alone.
| #
| # GNU Make 4.4.1 writes `a`, `b` with a space after it, and `c`.
| .ONESHELL:
| SP = $(subst x, ,x)
| all:
| 	@printf '%s' "a
| 	b$(SP)
| 	c" > out
